<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Population Registration Act, 1950</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Population Registration Act of 1950 required every South African to be classified and registered by race, as white, coloured or native, later black, with Indians added as a subgroup. Classification, decided by appearance, descent and social acceptance, determined where people could live, work and study under apartheid and divided families. It was the foundation of apartheid legislation until its repeal in 1991.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was formed in 1910, each of its four colonies and republics had its own rules for classifying race: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> by appearance and descent, in the Free State also by general acceptance, repute and living conditions, while Natal gave no criteria. In November 1948 Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/d-f-malan">D. F. Malan</a> told a Transvaal National Party congress that every race would be registered and given an identity card, to show where people might live and whom they might marry. The act worked alongside the Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act, 1949, and the Immorality Amendment Act, 1950.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>An Office for Race Classification oversaw the process, judging by appearance, general acceptance and social standing; to separate coloured from white people it looked at hair, skin colour, facial features, home language, friends, employment, socioeconomic status and even eating and drinking habits. Reclassification was common: in 1982, 1,189 people applied and 997 decisions were positive. Families were divided; in 1967 the two Dickson children, classified white, were expelled from their primary school after 42 of 45 sets of parents complained, and the whole family was reclassified coloured. Japanese residents were treated as honorary whites, partly because of trade, and the act was amended many times to close loopholes.</p><p>From 1953 to 1972 South Africans and permanent residents carried an identity card with a number ending in a letter for race; black people's cards were glued into their reference books. From 1972 the Book of Life replaced it, with the register kept from 1971 on an IBM mainframe in the Civitas building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Parliament repealed the act on 17 June 1991, with 38 votes against, all from the Conservative Party. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk">F. W. de Klerk</a> said the government was unqualified in its commitment to removing racial discrimination from every law, and in July 1991 President George H. W. Bush lifted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comprehensive-anti-apartheid-act">Comprehensive Anti-Apartheid Act</a> of 1986. Its categories remained in use in policies meant to redress past imbalances, such as the Employment Equity Act of 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Population Registration Act, 1950” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Population Registration Act, 1950</b>, classified every person by race; the Group Areas Act of 1950 assigned separate residential areas to each group; the Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act of 1949 and the Immorality Act banned marriage and sex across the colour line; the Suppression of Communism Act…</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
